@$VER: WaveSmith.guide 1.0 (15.11.2026)
@author "Marcus Gerards"
@wordwrap

@node Main "WaveSmith"

@{b}WaveSmith@{ub} - a recording's ends trimmed by eye and ear

WaveSmith opens a recording, finds where the music starts and ends,
and shows the lead-in and the run-out shaded, In and Out set before
and after them. Listen to both ends, drag the handles if the guess was
not right, and Save: the file is written anew without the shaded
parts. It also cuts parts out, splits a side into its tracks at
markers and writes a cue sheet for them.

  @{" Requirements                                        " link Requirements}
  @{" Starting      - from Workbench or the Shell         " link Starting}
  @{" The window    - its parts, the status line          " link Window}
  @{" The waveform  - zooming, scrolling, the mouse       " link Waveform}
  @{" Trimming      - In, Out, the suggestion             " link Trimming}
  @{" Listening     - Play, Listen, Loop, card or AHI     " link Listening}
  @{" Editing       - Cut out, Undo, Split at the cursor  " link Editing}
  @{" Markers       - tracks, Split at markers, cue sheet " link Markers}
  @{" Saving        - Save, Save as, Next, the files      " link Saving}
  @{" Settings      - the Settings menu                   " link Settings}
  @{" Menus                                               " link Menus}
  @{" Keys                                                " link Keys}
  @{" Messages      - when something goes wrong           " link Messages}
  @{" History                                             " link History}
  @{" Thanks                                              " link Thanks}
  @{" Licence                                             " link Licence}

The Help key opens the page about what is under the mouse pointer.
Rest the pointer for a moment on a button, a handle or the sound, and
a bubble says what it is.

@endnode

@node Requirements "WaveSmith: Requirements"

@{b}Requirements@{ub}

  - AmigaOS 3.2 (ReAction)
  - for playing: a Prisma Megamix card with prisma.library 3 or newer,
    or AHI (ahi.device 4 or newer)
  - for files other than WAV (AIFF, FLAC, MP3, Ogg Vorbis, AAC): the
    card and prismadsp.library 1.1 or newer, which decodes them

WAV files are read as they are: 16-bit PCM, mono or stereo. The sound
stays in the file while WaveSmith works on it; only its picture is
kept in memory, so a whole record side of 25 minutes is no trouble.

@endnode

@node Starting "WaveSmith: Starting"

@{b}Starting@{ub}

The Installer in WaveSmith's package (double-click Install) puts it
into a drawer of its own, SYS:Tools/WaveSmith unless told otherwise,
with this manual in Help and the German translation in Catalogs; it
offers the GlowIcons or the classic icons. By hand, copy the drawer
Files/WaveSmith from the package anywhere. Nothing goes into LIBS:,
DEVS: or S:.

From Workbench: WaveSmith as a sound file's default tool, or a click
on WaveSmith's icon with a sound file's icon shift-clicked. A sound
file's icon dropped on the open window is opened as well.

From the Shell:

  WaveSmith [FILE] <file> [PUBSCREEN <name>]

  FILE       the sound file to open
  PUBSCREEN  the public screen to open on (Workbench's otherwise)

Without a file the window opens empty: Project > Open... picks one.

The file is read a piece at a time and the window can be used at once.
A long file's ends are read first, so that In and Out are there before
the rest of the picture.

@endnode

@node Window "WaveSmith: The window"

@{b}The window@{ub}

From the top:

  - the @{"overview" link Waveform}: the whole file, In and Out, a box
    round the part the waveform shows
  - the dotted weight bar: dragged down, the overview grows and the
    waveform shrinks; Settings > Save settings keeps its place
  - the @{"waveform" link Waveform}: the part of the file in view, with
    a time ruler, one lane per channel
  - the scroller, as long as the waveform
  - the bar: the @{"deck" link Listening} and @{"Loop" link Listening};
    the @{"flags" link Trimming} In and Out; a read-out of how long the
    part kept is; @{"Trim..." link Trimming}; the
    @{"lenses" link Waveform}: zoom out, zoom in, the whole file
  - the status line, Cancel beside it

The title bar names the file, its length, its rate and its channels.
A * after the name: there are changes that are not saved yet.

The status line says what WaveSmith found and did. While a file is
read, decoded or saved it fills from the left, says the per cent and
the time left, and Cancel (or Esc) calls the job off.

@endnode

@node Waveform "WaveSmith: The waveform"

@{b}The waveform and the overview@{ub}

The waveform shows a part of the file; the overview above it shows all
of it. In both the lead-in and the run-out (outside In and Out) are
shaded.

@{b}Zooming@{ub}
  The lenses at the bar's right: zoom out, zoom in and the whole file
  (Right-Amiga -, + and 0). A handle's flag in the bar shows 3 seconds
  around that handle (View > Go to In and Go to Out too). The mouse
  wheel over the waveform zooms where the pointer is: away from you
  in, towards you out. Up and Down zoom in and out too.

@{b}Scrolling@{ub}
  The scroller; Shift and the wheel; in the overview a click puts the
  view there, and its box can be dragged. While the sound plays, the
  view turns a page when the play head runs off it.

@{b}The mouse in the waveform@{ub}
  A click puts the play marker there (while playing, the sound jumps
  there). A drag over the sound marks a part (View > Zoom to selection
  fills the view with it). In and Out are dragged by their lines or
  their flags, a marker by its line.

View > dB scale? draws the level in decibels: quiet parts look bigger.

@endnode

@node Trimming "WaveSmith: Trimming"

@{b}Trimming@{ub}

When a file is open WaveSmith looks for the music: the lead-in's
groove noise is measured and the music starts where it gets clearly
louder (a record's crackle does not fool it). In is put half a second
before the music, Out two seconds after its last sound, so a fade
stays whole. The status line says what was found: the lead-in and the
run-out, their clicks, the run-out groove's click every so often. No
music found: In and Out stay at the file's ends.

The handles move by:

  - a drag of their line or flag, in the waveform or the overview
  - the cursor keys, once a handle was dragged, nudged or picked by
    its flag in the bar (see @{"Keys" link Keys})
  - the Trim window: a time typed (m:ss.mmm) and Return, or [<] and
    [>], a step that follows the zoom

A handle let go snaps to the nearest zero crossing, so the cut does
not click (Settings > Snap to zero crossings? turns that off).

@{b}The flags in the bar@{ub}
  In and Out, in the handles' colours: a click shows 3 seconds around
  that handle, and the cursor keys move it from then on. The flag
  pressed in is the handle last touched, in the waveform too.

@{b}The Trim window@{ub}
  Trim... in the bar, Edit > Trim... (Right-Amiga T) or a double-click
  on a handle's flag in the waveform opens it. For In and for Out: the
  time, [<] and [>], Listen, the fade's length (Effects > Fade in and
  Fade out) and Show, which turns the view to the handle. Listen at In
  plays 3 seconds from In, Listen at Out the last 3 seconds up to Out.
  The window stays open beside the main one until its close gadget.

The read-out in the bar, between the flags and Trim..., says how long
the part kept is (an hour and more to the tenth of a second); its
bubble says the fades too.

How far before and after the music In and Out go is
Settings > @{"Lead and tail..." link Settings}.

@endnode

@node Listening "WaveSmith: Listening"

@{b}Listening@{ub}

The deck in the bar:

  <<       Rewind: the play marker to the stop before it -- a marker,
           In, the start of the file
  >        Play: the part marked, else from the play marker to Out
  >>       Forward: the play marker to the next stop -- a marker, Out,
           the end of the file
  Stop     stops, the play marker back to the start
  ||       Pause: the play held; again, it plays on
  Loop     the key beside the deck, on: what plays starts again at its
           end, until Stop -- the part marked, else In to Out (past
           Out: to the end)

Space is Play and Pause, Shift Space plays the part marked and only
it. The Play menu has these too, Listen at In and Listen at Out, and
Loop?, which is the window's Loop: switching one switches the other.
Loop is off at every start.

The play head runs through the overview and the waveform. Paused, it
stays where it was, and Play goes on from there.

@{b}Where it plays@{ub}
  Settings > Play through: Automatic (the card, else AHI), The Prisma
  card, or AHI. Automatic plays through AHI when there is no card, and
  when another program has the card. Settings > AHI mode... picks the
  mode and the rate in AHI's own requester; AHI's default is its own
  preferences' mode. Settings > Save settings keeps both.

@endnode

@node Editing "WaveSmith: Editing"

@{b}Editing@{ub}

@{b}Cut out@{ub} (Right-Amiga X): drag over a part, then choose
  Join     the parts on either side closed up, a thin mark where the
           gap was
  Silence  silence in its place, the length as it was

@{b}Undo@{ub} (Right-Amiga Z) and @{b}Redo@{ub} (Right-Amiga Y): every
cut, every move of In or Out and every marker change is a step. A run
of nudges with a key held down is one step. Undone back to the file as
it was opened, the * in the title goes.

Edit > Select all and Select none mark the whole file or nothing.

@{b}Split at the cursor@{ub} (Project): for Side.wav it writes Side-1.wav
from In to the play marker and Side-2.wav from the play marker to Out,
beside the file, the cuts in them. The file and the window stay as they were.

Nothing is written into the file before @{"Save" link Saving}.

@endnode

@node Markers "WaveSmith: Markers"

@{b}Markers@{ub}

A marker says where a track starts.

  Return or Enter   a marker at the play marker; tap it while the
                    sound plays
  a click on one    picks it; drag it to move it
  Del               takes the picked one away
  F1, F2            the play marker to the marker before or after
                    it, In and Out the ends of the way; while the
                    sound plays, it plays on from there (F1 within a
                    second after a marker goes to the one before)

The deck's Rewind and Forward stop at the markers too, and past In
and Out at the file's start and end.

Edit > Find tracks puts one at each track's start, found from the
breaks between them; a long side is measured first (the status line
fills, Cancel calls it off). Undo takes every change back.

Project > Split at markers writes the tracks between In and Out
beside the file: for Side.wav, Side-01.wav, Side-02.wav and on.

Save writes the markers as a cue sheet beside the file, Side.cue, and
they come back from it when the file is opened again. SplitTracks
CUE Side.cue cuts the side at them too.

@endnode

@node Saving "WaveSmith: Saving"

@{b}Saving@{ub}

Save (Right-Amiga S) writes the file anew without the shaded parts and
the parts cut out. The status line counts the per cent; Cancel or Esc
calls it off, and the file stays as it was. Saved, the file is read
again and Undo starts anew.

Save as... (Right-Amiga A) writes a new file, and WaveSmith works on
that one from then on; the original stays as it was. A name that is
there is asked about first.

A file that is not a WAV (FLAC, MP3, ...) is decoded first into a work
file beside it, Side.flac.wsmith.wav for Side.flac, which goes when the
file is closed. Save asks for a new name then, Side-trim.wav filled
in.

What Save writes beside Side.wav:

  Side.wav.bak   the old file, with Settings > Keep a backup? on
                 (and Side.prl.bak)
  Side.prl       PrismaRec's level map, when there is one, cut as
                 the sound was
  Side.cue       the cue sheet, when there are markers
  Side.wav.info  for a new file, with Settings > Create icons? on

Project > Open next opens the next sound file in the same drawer, by
name: a side's tracks trimmed one after the other.

Project > Revert reads the file again, the changes gone. Quitting or
opening another file with changes not saved asks first: Save, Discard
or Cancel.

@endnode

@node Settings "WaveSmith: Settings"

@{b}Settings@{ub}

@{b}Play through@{ub}: Automatic, The Prisma card or AHI; see
@{"Listening" link Listening}.

@{b}AHI mode...@{ub}: the mode and the rate WaveSmith plays through AHI
in.

@{b}Snap to zero crossings?@{ub} (on): a handle let go moves to the
nearest zero crossing.

@{b}Lead and tail...@{ub}: how far In goes before the music (0 to 10,000
ms, 500 at first) and Out after it (0 to 30,000 ms, 2000 at first).
Slide or type the milliseconds; the seconds beside them follow. Use
moves In and Out to the new places while nothing was changed yet;
otherwise the next file gets them.

@{b}Keep a backup?@{ub} (off): Save keeps the old file, Side.wav as
Side.wav.bak.

@{b}Create icons?@{ub} (on): every new file gets the system's icon for a
sound.

@{b}Save settings@{ub}: these, and the weight bar's place. They are read
from WaveSmith.prefs beside the program when there is one, and saved
back there; else from ENV: and saved to ENVARC: and ENV:.

@endnode

@node Menus "WaveSmith: Menus"

@{b}Menus@{ub}

  Project   Open... (O), Open next, Save (S), Save as... (A),
            Split at the cursor..., Split at markers..., Revert,
            Help..., About... (?), Quit (Q)
  Edit      Undo (Z), Redo (Y), Cut out... (X), Select all,
            Select none, Add marker (Return), Remove marker (Del),
            Find tracks, Trim... (T)
  View      Zoom in (+), Zoom out (-), Show all (0),
            Zoom to selection (Shift Z), Go to In, Go to Out,
            Previous marker (F1), Next marker (F2), dB scale?
  Effects   Fade in (F), Fade out (Shift F), Gain... (G), Normalise,
            Remove fades and gain
  Play      Play/Pause (Space), Play selection (Shift Space), Stop,
            Listen at In, Listen at Out, Loop?
  Settings  Play through, AHI mode..., Snap to zero crossings?,
            Lead and tail..., Keep a backup?, Create icons?,
            Save settings

The letters are Right-Amiga keys.

@endnode

@node Keys "WaveSmith: Keys"

@{b}Keys@{ub}

  Space             Play, Pause
  Shift Space       play the part marked
  Return, Enter     a marker at the play marker
  Del               the picked marker away
  F1, F2            the marker before, after the play marker
  Esc               call off reading, decoding or saving
  Help              this manual, at the page about what is under
                    the pointer
  Left, Right       the play marker a column of the view on or back;
                    with Shift a page, with Ctrl to the start or the
                    end
  Up, Down          zoom in, zoom out

Once In or Out was dragged or nudged, Left and Right move that handle
instead, until the next click on the sound. Held down, the moves are
one step for Undo.

The menus' keys: see @{"Menus" link Menus}.

@endnode

@node Messages "WaveSmith: Messages"

@{b}When something goes wrong@{ub}

@{b}... is no 16-bit WAV: other formats need prismadsp.library 1.1@{ub} -
install the driver package; the card decodes them.

@{b}Only 16-bit PCM WAV files, mono or stereo@{ub} - other WAV files
(8 or 24 bits, more channels) are not read yet.

@{b}The card is in use by ...@{ub} - a player holds the card: stop it,
or play through AHI (Settings > Play through).

@{b}Nothing to play through@{ub} - neither prisma.library 3 nor
ahi.device 4 is installed.

@{b}AHI's mode is not there or in use@{ub} - pick another one with
Settings > AHI mode...

@{b}Saving failed: DOS error ...@{ub} - the file is as it was.

@{b}Saving failed half-way@{ub} - the trimmed sound is kept, Side.wav's
as Side.wav.wsmith; the message says where the original is.

@{b}Too many cuts: save first@{ub} - Save, then go on cutting.

@endnode

@node History "WaveSmith: History"

@{b}History@{ub}

1.0 (15.11.2026) - the first public release: a package whose Installer
puts WaveSmith into a drawer of its own; free software under the GNU
Lesser General Public License; the About window says what WaveSmith
does.

0.4 (6.11.2026) - the new name (it was PrismaEdit); one bar under the
sound: the deck, Loop, the flags In and Out, the length, Trim... and the
lenses; the Trim window; F1 and F2 from marker to marker; the play
head a sprite, steady on slow machines; fades and gain drawn in the
lanes; pages and new views filled in by pieces, the controls answered
first (an A2000 too); without a card and AHI WaveSmith says so; the
GlowIcon and the About picture by Martin "Mason" Merz.

0.3 (27.10.2026) - playing through AHI, German, this manual and the Help
key, the Effects menu: Fade in, Fade out, fades at In and Out, Gain...,
Normalise, Remove fades and gain.

0.2 (27.10.2026) - playing through the card, files other than WAV
decoded on the card, the mouse wheel, Cut out, Undo and Redo, Split at
the cursor, the weight bar, the Settings menu, Save as, the Play menu
with Loop, the cursor keys, markers, Find tracks, Split at markers and
cue sheets.

0.1 (26.10.2026) - the first version: a 16-bit WAV opened, In and Out
suggested, the handles dragged, nudged or typed, Save and Next.

@endnode

